  "raw": {
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Program-Coodinator---Course-Materials--Falcon-Advantage-_JR100374": {
      "title": "Program Coodinator - Course Materials (Falcon Advantage)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are   Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community.   What We Need   Seattle Pacific University is   seeking   a highly organized and service-focused   Program Coordinator – Course Materials (Falcon Advantage)   to oversee the day-to-day administration and   logistics   of the university’s course materials program.     Falcon Advantage supports students and faculty by helping ensure required course materials are available accurately, affordably, and on time. Through strong coordination with faculty, publishers, vendors, and campus partners, the program helps create a smoother academic experience and supports student readiness from the first day of class.   This position serves as the primary point of contact for faculty, students, campus departments, and publishing partners. The Program Coordinator manages course material adoptions, procurement, inventory coordination, student communications, billing processes, vendor relationships, and program reporting to help ensure required materials are delivered accurately and on time.   The   Program Coordinator   supports Seattle Pacific  University’s mission  by contributing to a  Christ centered  learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional  expertise  to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees  are required to  be aligned with and supportive of  SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.   The ideal candidate is detail-oriented, dependable, and comfortable managing multiple deadlines in a fast-paced academic environment. This role requires   strong communication , problem-solving, follow-through, and the ability to develop efficient processes that improve the experience for students, faculty, and university partners.   This is an   on-campus position based at Seattle Pacific University in Seattle, WA .   What   You’ll   Do   Course Materials & Program Coordination   Coordinate the end-to-end   logistics   of the Falcon Advantage course materials program   Communicate quarterly course material adoption deadlines and reminders to faculty   Support late adoptions, changes, and other time-sensitive course material needs   Coordinate physical and digital course material delivery to help ensure students have access by the first day of class   Manage returns, uncollected materials, and refund processes with vendors   Procurement, Inventory & Vendor Management   Coordinate purchasing of physical course materials with publishers and vendors   Monitor ordering timelines, shipment status, and inventory needs   Maintain   accurate   records of physical course materials and related inventory   Build and   maintain   effective working relationships with publishers, distributors, and other external partners   Resolve order, delivery, billing, and inventory issues in   a timely   and service-oriented manner   Faculty & Student Support   Serve as a primary resource for faculty and students   regarding   Falcon Advantage processes and deadlines   Meet with new faculty to provide orientation and training on course material adoptions   Consult with faculty   regarding   course material options and adoption processes   Respond to student, faculty, and vendor questions by phone, email, and in person   Send quarterly student communications   regarding   program deadlines, access, billing, and opt-out processes   Billing, Budget & Program Operations   Process invoices for course materials and help   monitor   annual program expenses and revenue   Establish and   maintain   student billing parameters for Falcon Advantage charges   Coordinate refunds and billing adjustments as needed   Maintain   accurate   program records and support reporting and reconciliation processes   Identify   opportunities to improve program efficiency, communication, and service delivery   SPU Store & University Support   Assist with SPU Store operations during slower Falcon Advantage periods and high-volume events   Provide support during major university events such as Orientation and Commencement   Participate   in regular meetings with the Falcon Advantage/SPU Store Manager and Office of Business & Finance   Participate in departmental activities, professional development, goal setting, and university service as appropriate   What You Bring   Bachelor’s degree or   equivalent   combination of education and relevant professional experience in inventory management, customer service, operations, retail, or   related   field.   Demonstrated experience with customer service and inventory management   Excellent written and verbal communication skills   Exceptional organization, attention to detail, and time-management skills   Ability to manage multiple deadlines and priorities in a fast-paced environment   Strong problem-solving skills and the ability to follow issues through to resolution   Ability to build productive working relationships with faculty, students, vendors, and campus partners   Proficiency   with Microsoft Office applications, including Word, Excel, and Outlook   Comfort learning and using adoption portals, inventory systems, and other business applications   Mission Alignment:   A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting   SPU’s mission ; alignment with the   University’s Statement of Faith;   and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community.   Preferred Qualifications   Experience working in higher education, a college or university bookstore, publishing, retail operations, or course materials   Experience coordinating textbooks, digital course materials, equitable-access programs, open educational resources, or other academic course materials   Experience with purchasing, vendor management, inventory, order fulfillment, or program   logistics   Compensation & Position Details   Hourly Rate:   $24.04–$26.44 per hour, depending on experience   Status:   Full-time, non-exempt, this position may require occasional weekend work during major university events, including Orientation and Commencement.    Department:   Office of Business & Finance   Location:   Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position)   Benefits:   SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents.   Additional Information: Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to   submit   the following materials:   Resume   Cover Letter   We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Office---Operations-Manager--Student-Counseling--Health--and-Wellness_JR100359": {
      "title": "Office & Operations Manager, Student Counseling, Health, and Wellness",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Student Counseling, Health & Wellness   (SCHW) supports the holistic well-being of SPU students by providing compassionate counseling, health services, prevention programming, and wellness education. Our team partners across the university to promote student success through accessible, student-centered care in a welcoming and confidential environment. What We Need Seattle Pacific University SCHW is seeking an organized, collaborative, and service-oriented   Office & Operations Manager   to provide operational leadership and administrative management for Student Counseling, Health & Wellness. This position is 32 hours per week for 10 months and 20 hours per week for two months in the summer. Reporting jointly to the Director of Student Counseling and the Director of Health Services, this position oversees the day-to-day administrative operations of the department while ensuring a welcoming experience for students and visitors. The Office & Operations Manager coordinates office operations, supervises administrative support staff and graduate assistants, supports budgeting and purchasing, oversees department technology and records management, assists with communications and outreach, and helps ensure compliance with HIPAA, FERPA, and university policies. The ideal candidate is highly organized, enjoys building efficient systems, exercises excellent judgment with confidential information, and thrives in a collaborative environment that supports student well-being. The Office & Operations Manager supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission  by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. This is an   on-campus position based in Seattle, WA . What You'll Do Office & Administrative Operations Coordinate the daily administrative operations of Student Counseling, Health & Wellness Provide executive administrative support to the Directors of Student Counseling and Health Services Manage calendars, scheduling, correspondence, meeting coordination, reports, and departmental communications Help maintain a welcoming, professional, and responsive office environment for students, families, faculty, staff, and community partners Department Operations & Staff Support Coordinate onboarding for new staff, graduate assistants, and counseling interns Supervise administrative assistants and graduate assistants, including scheduling, training, workflow coordination, and performance support Assist with professional staff transitions and orientation activities Coordinate office procedures, documentation, and operational workflows that support efficient service delivery Financial & Business Operations Coordinate purchasing, procurement card transactions, payment requests, reconciliations, and budget tracking Assist departmental leadership with budget monitoring and financial reporting Manage office, clinical, laboratory, and medical supply inventories Coordinate billing and reimbursement activities for Health Services in partnership with departmental leadership Technology, Records & Compliance Coordinate department technology systems, software applications, and administrative databases Support the department's transition to electronic health records and ongoing records management Maintain clinical scheduling, case assignment, referral, and client record systems Help ensure compliance with HIPAA, FERPA, records retention requirements, privacy standards, and other applicable regulations Coordinate department technology projects and equipment needs Communications & Outreach Maintain department website content and assist with social media communications Coordinate campus communications, marketing materials, and outreach initiatives Partner with campus departments to support wellness programming, educational events, and community engagement activities Emergency Preparedness Support Student Counseling, Health & Wellness participation in university emergency response planning Coordinate departmental emergency drills and maintain emergency response supplies and documentation Assist departmental leadership in supporting campus crisis response activities as needed   What You Bring Bachelor's degree One year of experience in administrative support, office management, operations, or a related role Experience coordinating office systems, administrative processes, and daily operations Experience supervising or coordinating the work of others Strong organizational, communication, and customer service skills Ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining exceptional attention to detail Ability to exercise sound judgment and maintain confidentiality when working with sensitive information Experience working with college-aged students or in a student-centered environment Strong proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, including Outlook, Word, and Excel Ability to build trust while working with highly confidential health, student, and personnel information. Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of progressively responsible administrative or office management experience Supervisory experience Experience in higher education, healthcare, counseling, or a related setting Background or coursework in counseling, health services, student affairs, or a related field Experience working with electronic records systems, Workday, or other enterprise software Familiarity with HIPAA, FERPA, or healthcare privacy requirements   Compensation & Position Details Hourly Rate:   $26.00–$28.50 per hour, depending on experience Status:   Full-time, non-exempt Department:   Student Counseling, Health & Wellness Reports To:   Director of Student Counseling and Director of Health Services Location:   Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Work Schedule:   This position is   32 hours per week from September through June   and   20 hours per week July and August , reflecting the academic-year service model of Student Counseling, Health & Wellness. Benefits:   SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to supporting the health, wellness, and success of SPU students."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Chemistry-Laboratory-Coordinator--Full-time-10mos--part-time-2-mos-_JR100366": {
      "title": "Chemistry Laboratory Coordinator (Full time 10mos, part time 2 mos)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-10\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. What's our need? Seattle Pacific University is hiring a Chemistry Lab Coordinator to support the teaching mission of the Chemistry Department by ensuring that all instructional labs are prepared, safe, and well-equipped. This role is ideal for someone who thrives in a scientific setting, has a hands-on approach, and enjoys mentoring student workers. This position works full time for 10 months and then half time for 2 months during the summer. The Chemistry Lab Coordinator supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission  by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What will you do? As the Chemistry Lab Coordinator you will: Prepare solutions and set up equipment for chemistry experiments and labs. Supervise and train student Teaching Assistants. Ensure safety regulations and chemical hygiene protocols are followed in all instructional labs. Collect, log, and manage hazardous waste waste weekly. Maintain clean and organized lab spaces; perform minor repairs on instruments. Monitor lab inventory and coordinate orders for supplies and equipment with the Lab Manager. Collaborate with the Biology lab coordinator to coordinate shared equipment usage. Perform annual tasks such as a deep lab cleaning, physical inventory of chemicals and glassware, and complete required RCRA and DOT trainings. Assist with biology lab coordination and supply/equipment pickups as needed. Complete other duties as assigned by the Lab Manager What will you bring? We're looking for someone with: A Bachelor's degree in Chemistry. Demonstrated hands-on laboratory experience in a chemistry lab, either through academic coursework or professional roles. Familiarity with scientific equipment, including pH meters, balances, micropipettes, and Spectronic 20s. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work independently and as part of a collaborative team Comfort supervising and mentoring student workers. Physical ability to lift and carry 40 - 50 pounds. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and a willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Compensation and benefits: $24.00 - $25.50 per hour, DOE This position works full time for 10 months and then half time for 2 months during the summer. Chemistry coordinators are offered the SPU benefits package for full time employees that includes: Medical, dental, and vision coverage Retirement plan with generous university contribution Paid holidays, vacation and sick time Tuition assistance for employees and eligible dependents Additional Information: To apply: Please submit your application through our Workday Careers site. Be prepared to upload both a resume and cover letter. We look forward to hearing about how you can contribute to the learning environment of our amazing students and to SPU's mission to engage the culture and change the world!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Mental-Health-Counselor---Part-time_JR100361": {
      "title": "Mental Health Counselor - Part time",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. The Student Counseling Center (SCC) supports students' emotional, relational, and psychological well-being through compassionate counseling services, crisis response, consultation, prevention programming, and collaborative care. We are committed to helping students thrive academically, personally, and spiritually while fostering a caring campus community. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking a part-time compassionate and clinically skilled Mental Health Counselor to join the Student Counseling Center. This position provides individual, couples, family, and group counseling services for undergraduate students while supporting crisis response, prevention programming, and campus-wide mental health initiatives. Working collaboratively with Student Life and campus partners, the counselor helps promote student well-being through direct clinical services, outreach, consultation, and educational programming. The ideal candidate enjoys working with college students, demonstrates sound clinical judgment, values collaborative care, and is committed to supporting students from a wide range of backgrounds, life experiences, and perspectives.   The Mental Health Counselor supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission  by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You'll Do Clinical Counseling Services Provide individual, couples, family, and group counseling for undergraduate students Conduct intake assessments and develop appropriate treatment recommendations Provide referrals to community resources and specialized care when appropriate Maintain timely, accurate, and confidential clinical documentation Crisis Assessment & Student Support Provide crisis intervention and risk assessment for students experiencing urgent mental health concerns Consult with faculty, staff, and Student Life colleagues regarding student concerns when appropriate Collaborate with campus partners to promote student safety and well-being Outreach & Prevention Develop and facilitate mental health education, wellness, and relationship-focused programs for students Participate in campus outreach initiatives that increase awareness of counseling services and mental health resources Support Student Counseling Center events and prevention programming throughout the academic year Professional Collaboration Participate in individual supervision, staff meetings, case consultation, and professional development Maintain compliance with professional ethical standards, Washington State licensing requirements, FERPA, and university policies Contribute to a collaborative, supportive counseling team focused on student success What You Bring Master's degree in Marriage and Family Therapy, Mental Health Counseling, Social Work, or a Doctorate in Clinical Psychology At least one year of counseling experience in a college counseling center or comparable setting Washington State licensure (or associate licensure) as an LMFT, LMHC, LICSW, or Licensed Clinical Psychologist Strong clinical assessment, counseling, and crisis intervention skills Experience supporting college-aged students or young adults Ability to work effectively with students from a wide variety of cultural, socioeconomic, faith, and life backgrounds Excellent interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills Commitment to maintaining professional ethics, confidentiality, and collaborative care Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission ; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Compensation & Position Details Hourly Rate: $26.00 - $27.50 per hour, DOE Status: Part-time, non-exempt FTE: 20 hours per week Appointment: Nine-month academic-year position (mid-September through mid-June) Department: Student Counseling Center Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Benefits: SPU pays a portion of the employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers paid vacation and sick time. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings can contribute to the health and well-being of SPU students while supporting the University's Christ-centered mission."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Pool--Biology_JR100030": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Biology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-27\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Department of Biology: The Biology Department at Seattle Pacific University is a place where students and faculty engage collaboratively in constructing and refining their understanding of the biological world. All biology courses emphasize the active participation of students in the learning process. We prioritize a highly interactive community of learning both inside and outside the classroom. Job Description: We typically have openings for adjunct biology faculty in multiple sections of non-majors biology, introductory biology, and anatomy & physiology. Teaching in these courses would provide an opportunity to work closely with other SPU faculty, with the intention of integrating authentic research experiences for students into the general biology lab curriculum. Requirements: Master's degree in biology or relevant field Bachelor's degree applicants will be considered Commitment to support active, participatory learning experiences Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting   SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the community. Additional Information: Compensation: Starts at $900 per credit The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific teaching needs arise. Please complete the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/resume. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Cara Wall-Scheffler, Ph.D. Professor and Co-Chair of Biology 206-281-2201 cwallsch@spu.edu Dr. Jenny Tenlen, Ph.D. Associate Professor and Co-Chair of Biology (206) 281-2007 tenlenj@spu.edu"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/IT-Project-Manager_JR100354": {
      "title": "IT Project Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-27\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Information Technology supports this mission by delivering secure, reliable, and forward-looking technology that enables students, faculty, and staff to do their best work. SPU IT values service, stewardship, collaboration, transparency, continuous improvement, and thoughtful innovation in support of the university’s academic and operational priorities. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking an experienced and collaborative IT Project Manager, for an on-campus position, to lead technology projects and initiatives that support institutional priorities and organizational change. As a member of the IT Business Systems team, this position independently plans and executes university technology projects, coordinates cross-functional project teams, maintains the IT project portfolio, and helps ensure project outcomes align with the university’s strategic goals. The IT Project Manager also partners with campus departments to understand business needs, translate complex requirements, and support enterprise technology systems and processes. The ideal candidate combines strong project leadership with business analysis, communication, relationship-building, and technical aptitude. This person is comfortable navigating ambiguity, coordinating diverse stakeholders, anticipating risk, and turning complex needs into practical and sustainable outcomes. The IT Project Manager supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. This is an on-campus position based at Seattle Pacific University in Seattle, WA. What You’ll Do Program & Project Leadership Independently lead the planning, review, and execution of university technology projects and initiatives Maintain and coordinate IT’s project portfolio in partnership with department leadership Define and manage project scope, schedules, milestones, dependencies, resources, risks, and outcomes Lead project meetings and maintain clear, timely communication across project teams and stakeholders Identify and resolve roadblocks while escalating risks and decisions appropriately Ensure project outcomes align with university priorities, business needs, and available resources Apply modern project-management tools and practices to strengthen IT project methodologies and delivery Business Systems Analysis & Stakeholder Partnership Build strong, constructive relationships with departments and business units across the university Develop an understanding of stakeholder operations, strategic goals, workflows, data, and technology systems Facilitate discovery and requirements-gathering conversations with technical and nontechnical partners Translate complex business needs into clear functional and technical requirements Identify opportunities to improve processes, systems, service delivery, and user experience Present context, options, risks, and recommendations to support effective decision-making Enterprise Systems Support Provide Tier II and Tier III assistance, triage, research, and troubleshooting for enterprise technology systems and processes Assist with the design, development, implementation, and improvement of enterprise technology solutions Support administration and ongoing operation of enterprise systems Coordinate testing, implementation, communication, and change-management activities for technology updates Partner with Business Systems Analysts and other IT colleagues to support reliable and sustainable operations Documentation, Standards & Governance Create and maintain project documentation, system documentation, policies, standards, and operating procedures Clarify roles, responsibilities, decision points, and support expectations across projects and operational processes Promote consistent documentation practices that support knowledge-sharing and long-term continuity Help establish repeatable processes that improve accountability, transparency, and service delivery Continuous Improvement & Team Contribution Stay current with project-management practices, tools, technologies, and industry trends Evaluate and introduce appropriate methods that improve project delivery and organizational effectiveness Incorporate lessons learned into IT standards, methodologies, and future project planning Collaborate across technical disciplines and accept matrix-style assignments based on institutional priorities, team needs, and individual strengths What You Bring Demonstrated experience leading technology projects involving multiple stakeholders, systems, or business units Experience using project-management, collaboration, and documentation platforms such as Jira, Microsoft Project, Smartsheet, Teams, SharePoint, or Confluence Strong knowledge of project-management principles, tools, methodologies, and risk-management practices Experience gathering, documenting, and translating business requirements into functional or technical solutions Ability to develop trusted working relationships with both technical and nontechnical stakeholders Strong written, verbal, facilitation, and presentation skills Ability to manage multiple projects, priorities, timelines, and dependencies Strong analytical, problem-solving, and organizational skills Sound judgment and the ability to navigate ambiguity, competing priorities, and organizational change Experience developing policies, procedures, standards, or technical documentation Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative, matrix-style team environment Service-oriented approach with a commitment to stewardship, continuous improvement, and dependable follow-through Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU’s mission; alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community Bachelor’s degree in information technology, business, project management, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and professional experience Preferred Qualifications Experience managing technology projects in higher education or another complex, mission-driven organization Experience supporting enterprise applications, business systems, or system implementations Experience with business-process improvement, organizational change management, or systems analysis Experience maintaining a project portfolio or supporting project-governance processes Project-management certification such as PMP, CAPM, Scrum, Agile, or a comparable credential Familiarity with IT service-management practices and Tier II or Tier III enterprise support Compensation & Position Details Salary Range: $85,000–$95,000 annually, depending on experience Status: Full-time, exempt Department: Information Technology Location: On-campus at Seattle Pacific University in Seattle, WA; this is not a remote position Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting, as eligible; and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Foundation-Accountant_JR100351": {
      "title": "Foundation Accountant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-23\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. The Seattle Pacific Foundation partners with Seattle Pacific University to steward charitable gifts, endowments, trusts, and investments that advance the University's mission for generations to come. Through sound financial management and careful stewardship, the Foundation supports scholarships, academic programs, faculty, and strategic initiatives that make a lasting impact on students and the broader SPU community. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking a detail-oriented and analytical Accountant to join the Seattle Pacific Foundation finance team. This position plays an important role in maintaining the financial integrity of both Seattle Pacific Foundation and Seattle Pacific University by supporting accounting, investment reporting, endowment and trust accounting, financial reporting, audits, tax filings, and board operations. The ideal candidate enjoys complex accounting work, has exceptional attention to detail, and values accuracy, collaboration, and stewardship. This position offers a unique opportunity to work with investment portfolios, charitable trusts, endowments, nonprofit accounting, and university finance within a mission-driven organization. The Foundation Accountant  supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission  by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You'll Do Accounting & Financial Reporting Perform accounting for Foundation and University investment, trust, endowment, and financial activities Prepare journal entries, reconciliations, adjustments, and closing entries Maintain accurate accounting records in accordance with Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) Support monthly, quarterly, and annual financial reporting activities Investments, Endowments & Trust Accounting Maintain accounting records for Foundation investment pools, endowments, and charitable trusts Record investment income, market activity, capital gains, distributions, and other investment transactions Assist with trust accounting and financial reporting for beneficiaries and trustors Monitor investment activity and help ensure accurate valuation and reporting Audit, Tax & Compliance Assist in preparing annual audited financial statements and supporting documentation Support preparation of IRS Form 990, trust tax returns, Washington State filings, and other regulatory reports Assist with annual audit activities and coordination with external auditors Help maintain strong internal controls to safeguard Foundation and University assets Foundation Board & Donor Support Assist in preparing reports and materials for Seattle Pacific Foundation Board meetings Coordinate Foundation Board and committee meeting logistics, minutes, and records Communicate professionally with donors, trustors, outside advisors, and board members Support annual trustor communications and stewardship activities Collaboration & Operational Excellence Partner with University Finance and Business & Finance teams to ensure accurate financial reporting Maintain a thorough understanding of Foundation accounting systems and financial processes Provide exceptional customer service while maintaining confidentiality and fiduciary responsibility Contribute to continuous improvement of accounting procedures and financial operations What You Bring Associate's or Bachelor's degree in Accounting or a related field 3-5 years of professional accounting experience Strong understanding of Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) Excellent analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills Advanced attention to detail and commitment to accuracy Strong Excel skills and experience using accounting software Excellent written and verbal communication skills Ability to work independently while contributing to a collaborative team environment High level of integrity, professionalism, discretion, and fiduciary responsibility Interest in investment accounting, endowments, trusts, or nonprofit financial management Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission ; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith ; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Accounting or a related field 5+ years of accounting experience CPA license or Accounting Certificate Experience with nonprofit, higher education, foundation, trust, or investment accounting Familiarity with investment accounting, charitable trusts, endowments, and multiple asset classes Experience with Workday, Argos, Banner, Adavico, or similar enterprise financial systems Compensation & Position Details Salary: $70,000 - $80,000, commensurate to experience Status: Full-time, exempt Department: Seattle Pacific Foundation Reports To: Foundation Director of Finance & Treasurer Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Rowing--Coach-II--Part-time--10-12-hours-per-week--_JR100347": {
      "title": "Assistant Rowing  Coach II (Part-time, 10-12 hours per week))",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-23\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Seattle Pacific Athletics is committed to developing champions in competition, in the classroom, and in life. As a member of NCAA Division II and the Great Northwest Athletic Conference (GNAC), SPU Athletics emphasizes excellence, integrity, service, and Christian character. The Women's Rowing program provides student-athletes with a highly competitive and supportive environment where they can grow as athletes, students, leaders, and followers of Christ. What We Need: Seattle Pacific University is seeking an enthusiastic and driven individual to join our Women’s Rowing team as a part-time Assistant Coach. This role is ideal for someone eager to grow within NCAA rowing while playing a pivotal role in recruiting, mentoring, and leading student-athletes toward competitive success. The ideal candidate will be a strong communicator, team player, and positive role model who thrives in a faith-centered educational environment. The Assistant Rowing Coach position supports Seattle Pacific University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You’ll Do: Lead, mentor, and support student-athletes with integrity and enthusiasm Recruit prospective student-athletes both from high schools and from the on-campus community Instruct rowing fundamentals and foster development of novice rowers into varsity contributors Collaborate with the Head Coach and other staff to deliver safe, successful team experiences Assist with administrative tasks and logistical operations Participate in spring travel and weekend events as required Help with rowing equipment maintenance and boathouse upkeep Represent SPU Athletics with professionalism and positivity   What You Bring: Education:  Bachelor’s degree required Experience:  Experience with a competitive collegiate rowing program (as athlete or coach) Skills & Characteristics: Strong interpersonal and leadership abilities Initiative and commitment to athlete development and recruitment Enthusiasm for collaborative team culture and student mentorship Interest in learning and growing as a collegiate coach Certifications (or willingness to obtain post-hire with SPU support): CPR Certification Washington State Boater’s License Washington State Driver’s License USRowing Level 2 Certification   Mission Alignment:  You have a personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Salary Range: $8,000 - $12,000 annually, DOE, 10 month appt Position Type: Part-Time, Exempt Hours: Approx. 10-12 hours per week; mornings preferred Start Date: Mid September End Date: Mid June Why SPU? Seattle Pacific University’s beautiful campus is nestled in North Queen Anne, just minutes from downtown Seattle. The Women’s Rowing program enjoys direct access to our on-campus boathouse and daily practices on the protected waters of the Seattle Ship Canal and Lake Union. SPU’s rowing team has experienced significant growth and success and is poised for continued excellence with institutional support and a strong vision for the future. Additional Information: Ready to Apply? Submit your application online through Workday, including a cover letter and resume. We look forward to hearing how you can contribute to the success and spirit of SPU Rowing."
    },
